      "text": "To determine the values of \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}\\), we first need to compute \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}\\).\n\nGiven the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe transpose of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute the product \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe calculate each element of the resulting matrix step by step.\n\n**First row, first column:**\n\\[\n0 \\cdot 0 + x \\cdot x + x \\cdot x = x^2 + x^2 = 2x^2\n\\]\n\n**First row, second column:**\n\\[\n0 \\cdot 2y + x \\cdot y + x \\cdot (-y) = 0 + xy - xy = 0\n\\]\n\n**First row, third column:**\n\\[\n0 \\cdot z + x \\cdot (-z) + x \\cdot z = 0 - xz + xz = 0\n\\]\n\n**Second row, first column:**\n\\[\n2y \\cdot 0 + y \\cdot x + (-y) \\cdot x = 0 + xy - xy = 0\n\\]\n\n**Second row, second column:**\n\\[\n2y \\cdot 2y + y \\cdot y + (-y) \\cdot (-y) = 4y^2 + y^2 + y^2 = 6y^2\n\\]\n\n**Second row, third column:**\n\\[\n2y \\cdot z + y \\cdot (-z) + (-y) \\cdot z = 2yz - yz - yz = 0\n\\]\n\n**Third row, first column:**\n\\[\nz \\cdot 0 + (-z) \\cdot x + z \\cdot x = 0 - zx + zx = 0\n\\]\n\n**Third row, second column:**\n\\[\nz \\cdot 2y + (-z) \\cdot y + z \\cdot (-y) = 2yz - yz - yz = 0\n\\]\n\n**Third row, third column:**\n\\[\nz \\cdot z + (-z) \\cdot (-z) + z \\cdot z = z^2 + z^2 + z^2 = 3z^2\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y^2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3z^2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}\\), we must have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y^2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n2x^2 = 1, \\quad 6y^2 = 1, \\quad 3z^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad y^2 = \\frac{1}{6}, \\quad z^2 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we find a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{6}, \\quad \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{2}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{6}{6} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
