      "text": "$\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + 2x^2 & xy + 2xy - y^2 & xz - yz + z^2 \\\\ xy + 2xy - y^2 & 4y^2 + y^2 + y^2 & 2yz - yz + z^2 \\\\ xz - yz + z^2 & 2yz - yz + z^2 & x^2 + 2x^2 + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3x^2 & 3xy - y^2 & xz - yz + z^2 \\\\ 3xy - y^2 & 6y^2 & yz + z^2 \\\\ xz - yz + z^2 & yz + z^2 & 3x^2 + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{I}.$\n\nTherefore, $3x^2 = 1,$ $6y^2 = 1,$ and $3x^2 + z^2 = 1.$  Then $x^2 = \\frac{1}{3},$ $y^2 = \\frac{1}{6},$ and $z^2 = \\frac{1}{3}.$  Hence, $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{11}{6}.$The answer is: \\frac{11}{6}",
