      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I},$ we have\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]This gives us\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + x^2 & 2xy - xy - z^2 & xz + xz - xz \\\\ 2xy - xy - z^2 & 4y^2 + y^2 + y^2 & 2yz - yz + yz \\\\ xz + xz - xz & 2yz - yz + yz & z^2 + z^2 + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2 & xy - z^2 & 0 \\\\ xy - z^2 & 6y^2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3z^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]From this, we see that\n\\begin{align*}\n2x^2 &= 1, \\\\\nxy - z^2 &= 0, \\\\\n6y^2 &= 1, \\\\\n3z^2 &= 1.\n\\end{align*}Then $x^2 = \\boxed{\\frac14},$ $y^2 = \\boxed{\\frac16},$ and $z^2 = \\boxed{\\frac13},$ so $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{\\frac7{12}}.$. The answer is \\frac{7}{12}",
